==============================================================================
§3  THE FLUX BRACKET, AND THE HYPOTHESIS THE BOX-WIDENING PLAN OMITTED
==============================================================================
  All numbers from the committed exact_f64 flux faces of VESSL 369367257597.
  r0 = plane_msl(lw drive) / box_net(lw drive)
  r1 = box_net(MSL drive)  / plane_msl(MSL drive)

  f [GHz]              1.00       1.75       2.50       3.25       4.00
  r0                0.97613    0.97784    0.97853    0.97883    0.97916
  r1                1.02566    1.02486    1.02484    1.02513    1.02535
  r1/r0             1.05074    1.04809    1.04733    1.04729    1.04718
  |S22| @ Zc        0.07742    0.08444    0.09496    0.10885    0.12525
  bracket @Zc      -0.04444   -0.04062   -0.03789   -0.03489   -0.03075
  bracket @ship    -0.05032   -0.04775   -0.04699   -0.04674   -0.04596
  bracket @|S|=0   -0.05074   -0.04809   -0.04733   -0.04729   -0.04718
  NEGATIVE at every bin at EVERY anchor, including |S22| = 0: no real |S00| closes it.

  (A) r1 > 1 IS NON-PHYSICAL ON ITS OWN — no box geometry needed to see it.
      In the MSL drive the only source sits at x = 5.52 mm, EAST of the MSL reference
      plane. Let R be everything west of that plane. The net inward flux across the plane
      IS the total time-averaged dissipation in R (resistor + whatever CPML lies in R);
      the net inward flux through the lw box IS the dissipation inside the box, and the
      box (x 1.76-2.24, y 1.26-1.74, z 0-0.494 mm) is a SUBREGION of R. Dissipation in a
      subregion cannot exceed dissipation in the region containing it, so r1 <= 1 with no
      losslessness assumption at all. Measured: 1.02484 .. 1.02566 — a 2.57% violation.
      Sign check on the plan's mechanism: a WIDER box captures MORE, so box_net grows on
      both drives; r0 = plane/box falls, r1 = box/plane rises, and r1/r0 rises as the
      square. Widening therefore makes r1 MORE non-physical and the bracket MORE negative.

  (B) A BOX-INDEPENDENT THIRD INSTRUMENT. The run also committed two FULL-cross-section
      x-planes at 1.44 mm and 2.56 mm (plane_mx / plane_px). They bound a slab containing
      the entire lw port and share NO face with the 5-face box.
      MSL drive, nested-region power budget. D(x<X) = net WESTWARD flux at X = the total
      dissipation west of X (the only source is at x = 5.52 mm). It must be >= 0 and must
      not DECREASE as X grows. Normalised to D(x<4.72 mm) = 1:
        D(x<1.44)    -0.00974   -0.01066   -0.01140   -0.01157   -0.01139   <-- NEGATIVE: 1.16% of the scale
        D(x<2.56)     1.00263    1.00212    1.00171    1.00150    1.00141   <-- EXCEEDS D(x<4.72) = 1
        D(box)        1.02566    1.02486    1.02484    1.02513    1.02535   <-- the box, a SUBREGION, exceeds both
      lw drive, same planes, source INSIDE the slab: eastward flux falls 2.3587e-28 ->
      2.3540e-28 from 2.56 to 4.72 mm (-0.20%, physical) and box_net >= the sum of the
      slab's two outflows. Every lw-drive budget is consistent; all three violations
      above are on the MSL drive only.

      box_net / (slab x-face budget), the tightest legitimate bound using both planes:
        lw drive     1.01345    1.01262    1.01223    1.01210    1.01194
        MSL drive    1.01312    1.01193    1.01158    1.01190    1.01238
      The same factor on two independent drives, to 0.069%. On the MSL drive the box is a
      SUBREGION of that slab, so this ratio MUST be <= 1 — it is a hard violation, the box
      reading 1.312% high. On the lw drive the source is inside and the same excess is
      degenerate with real y/z leakage, so it is not a violation there — only a match.
      Mechanism available at zero compute: the box's +/-x faces and its +y face are
      PIERCED BY PEC. The realized trace is y in [1.28, 1.76] mm while the box faces sit
      at y = 1.26 / 1.74 mm, centred on the DECLARED y_c = 1.50 mm, not the realized 1.52 —
      so one of the seven metal node rows is excluded asymmetrically and the face integrals
      are sampled at a PEC edge. That is a CLOSURE defect of the 5-face surface, not a
      width defect, and widening the faces does not remove it.

  (C) REPLACE THE BOX WITH THE PLANE BUDGET AND THE BRACKET IS STILL NEGATIVE.
      r0* = plane_msl(lw)/slab_out(lw); r1* = slab_in(MSL)/|plane_msl(MSL)|
      r0*               0.98926    0.99017    0.99049    0.99068    0.99085
      r1*               1.01238    1.01278    1.01311    1.01307    1.01281
      r1*/r0*           1.02337    1.02284    1.02283    1.02261    1.02216
      bracket* @Zc     -0.01723   -0.01554   -0.01361   -0.01049   -0.00613
      That removes 51-53% of the log-excess in r1/r0 and the bracket is STILL negative
      at all 5 bins, and r1* = 1.01311 is STILL > 1. So roughly half the anomaly is in the
      box and roughly half is in the plane instruments themselves.

  (D) WHAT BIAS MAGNITUDE WOULD BE NEEDED (the #838 family, asked and answered).
      r1/r0 = P_box(msl)*P_box(lw) / (P_plane_msl(msl)*P_plane_msl(lw)), so a COMMON
      multiplicative factor m on plane_msl (true = m x recorded) scales it by 1/m^2:
        m >= 1.0220  (2.20%) makes the bracket positive at all 5 bins
        m >= 1.0257  (2.57%) is independently required just to make r1 <= 1
      The two thresholds agree to 0.37% — the single bias that removes the non-physical
      gain in r1 also flips the bracket's sign, with NO box change at all.
      Equivalently a common factor b on box_net: b <= 0.9785 flips the bracket, b <= 0.9750
      makes r1 physical. After the box is replaced by the plane budget a residual
      m* >= 1.0086 (0.86%) is still required.
      HONEST CAVEAT ON #838: #838's measured ~3x over-read is in the MSL port's POWER-WAVE
      normalization (_b_msl = (V0 - Z_hj*I)/(2*sqrt(Z_hj))), and its own six-face flux box
      closed to -0.04/+0.06/+0.20%. plane_msl here is a POYNTING flux, not a power wave, so
      #838's stated mechanism does not bias it directly. What is measured above is a
      ~1.3% box-vs-plane and a ~1.2% plane-vs-plane POYNTING inconsistency. #838 remains
      the nearest open precedent for 'a constant in a port normalization', not a proven cause.

  (E) CONSEQUENCE FOR THE PROPOSED WIDER-BOX RUN: it must NOT be launched as designed.
      Net flux through a CLOSED surface in a source-free lossless region is invariant to
      widening it, which is exactly what a 2% two-box agreement gate asserts; and the
      under-capture mechanism has the wrong sign for both (A) and (D). A box run is
      justified only after someone names a mechanism by which a closed-surface flux moves
      by the ~2.2% the bracket needs. None is named here.

